{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: days 1–10 are close/slightly high; days ~11–22 tend to be biased high (model too elevated during the mid-horizon dip); days ~23–30 are biased low when a late rally occurs (model misses upside acceleration).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts—underestimates sharp upward breakouts late in the window (fails to capture momentum) and overestimates during subsequent drawdowns (keeps prices too high).",
    "Amplitude compression: forecasts are too smooth versus reality—underpredict highs after bullish breaks and (to a lesser extent) overpredict during troughs; apply a momentum-based widening of forecast range when recent realized volatility increases.",
    "Late-rally underprediction heuristic: if the last 3–7 realized days show a strong positive slope (e.g., >~0.6–0.8 EUR/day), add an increasing uplift to days 20–30 (ramping correction), as the model systematically undershoots late-window rallies.",
    "Mid-window mean-reversion overcall: when history shows a recent spike up, the model tends to hold the level too long; apply a small downward correction to days 10–22 unless momentum remains positive (reduce persistence)."
  ]
}
